# plans/plan_dx9.md D9-A5 scene corpus: envmap_fresnel_quad
# The first scene to genuinely exercise EnvironmentMapEffect's FRESNEL bucket with a real,
# non-degenerate per-vertex gradient (see envmap_quad.scene's own comment for why that scene,
# despite defaulting FresnelFactor=1, produces a spatially CONSTANT result -- its z=0-coplanar-
# with-eye geometry makes viewAngle=0 everywhere, so Fresnel-enabled and -disabled coincide).
#
# Real formula, confirmed against FNA's own EnvironmentMapEffect.fx (ComputeFresnelFactor,
# EnvironmentMapEffect.fx:61-66): `fresnelFactor = pow(max(1-abs(dot(eyeVector,worldNormal)),0),
# FresnelFactor) * EnvironmentMapAmount`, computed PER-VERTEX in the vertex shader (not
# recomputed per-pixel -- same Gouraud-interpolation discipline this corpus's own lighting/fog
# scenes already established: BasicEffect/EnvironmentMapEffect never use per-pixel lighting on
# this renderer, D9-82b/D9-82f's own "preferPerPixelLighting always false" finding), then linearly
# interpolated across the triangle like any other per-vertex quantity.
#
# World/View/Projection are Identity everywhere in this corpus, so EyePosition=(0,0,0) always.
# With the quad at z=0 (same plane as the eye), ANY single normal shared by all 4 vertices of
# this specific square-centered-at-origin geometry gives an identical fresnelFactor at all 4
# corners by symmetry (equal |x|,|y| at every corner) -- a real trap discovered while designing
# this scene: no single constant normal can produce a genuine gradient here. The fix is to
# deliberately assign DIFFERENT per-vertex normals to the top vs. bottom edge (a synthetic,
# non-physically-realistic choice made purely to isolate and prove the Fresnel formula itself --
# the same discipline this corpus already uses for e.g. its uniform-cubemap-color trick to
# sidestep hand-computing reflect() geometry):
#   Top edge    (y=0.6):  normal=(0,0,1) -> eyeVector.z=0 always (quad/eye coplanar) ->
#                         viewAngle=dot(eyeVector,normal)=0 -> fresnelFactor=pow(1,1)*1=1 exactly
#                         (full environment map, no Fresnel narrowing) at BOTH top vertices.
#   Bottom edge (y=-0.6): normal=(1,0,0) -> at (-0.6,-0.6,0): eyeVector=normalize(0.6,0.6,0)=
#                         (0.70711,0.70711,0), viewAngle=eyeVector.x=0.70711 ->
#                         fresnelFactor=pow(1-0.70711,1)*1=0.29289. At (0.6,-0.6,0):
#                         eyeVector=normalize(-0.6,0.6,0)=(-0.70711,0.70711,0),
#                         viewAngle=-0.70711, abs=0.70711 -> SAME fresnelFactor=0.29289 (both
#                         bottom corners agree by symmetry too) -- so top and bottom edges are
#                         each internally constant, but DIFFERENT from each other (1 vs 0.29289),
#                         giving a genuine, hand-derived, vertically-varying gradient once
#                         interpolated across the two triangles.
# lighting=true with light0enabled=false and ambientcolor=0,0,0 (light1/light2 default disabled)
# forces diffuseSum=0, so texture*diffuseSum=0 (black) regardless of texture content -- meaning
# result_rgb = lerp(0, environmentMap, fresnelFactor) = fresnelFactor * environmentMapColor
# exactly, with no lighting math to disentangle from the Fresnel math itself.
# Expected (environmentmappixel=200,100,50, environmentmapamount=1, fresnelfactor=1 exponent):
#   top edge    -> exactly (200,100,50)      [fresnelFactor=1]
#   bottom edge -> ~0.29289 * (200,100,50) = ~(58.6,29.3,14.6)  [fresnelFactor=0.29289]
# (exact byte values depend on GPU float rounding -- confirmed identical on both sides via the
# pixel diff, not independently re-derived byte-for-byte here.)
width=256
height=256
profile=HiDef
clearcolor=100,149,237,255
effect=EnvironmentMapEffect
vertexformat=PositionNormalTexture
lighting=true
ambientcolor=0,0,0
light0enabled=false
texture=true
texturewidth=1
textureheight=1
texturefilter=Point
environmentmap=true
environmentmapsize=1
environmentmapamount=1
fresnelfactor=1
primitive=TriangleList
texturepixel=255,255,255,255
environmentmappixel=200,100,50,255
# x,y,z,nx,ny,nz,u,v -- top edge normal=(0,0,1) [fresnelFactor=1 exactly], bottom edge
# normal=(1,0,0) [fresnelFactor=0.29289 by hand-derivation above].
vertex=-0.6,0.6,0,0,0,1,0,0
vertex=0.6,0.6,0,0,0,1,1,0
vertex=-0.6,-0.6,0,1,0,0,0,1
vertex=0.6,0.6,0,0,0,1,1,0
vertex=0.6,-0.6,0,1,0,0,1,1
vertex=-0.6,-0.6,0,1,0,0,0,1
